s/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/sam-definition.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: workflow definition; documents `sam_plan create` registration | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/plan-validator.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: validates plan structure; may need to handle incremental-state plans | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/scope.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: indirect reference to `sam_plan create` | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests_sam/test_server.py` - has `test_sam_create_*` suite (lines 515-809+); no `append_task` tests exist; new action needs integration tests for the happy path, duplicate task ID rejection, and plan-not-found | Risk: HIGH | Why: Zero coverage for the new `append` action path through the MCP server; the feature can ship without any failing tests but with no verified behavior\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests_sam/test_consolidated_tools.py` - exercises `task_backend.create_plan` directly on all backends; must be extended for `append_task` on all three backends | Risk: HIGH | Why: Backend conformance is validated here; without `append_task` tests, a backend that silently no-ops the append would pass CI\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_task_backend_conformance.py` - Protocol conformance suite; `append_task_section` is covered (line 283); `append_task` must be added as a conformance case to ensure all backends satisfy the Protocol | Risk: HIGH | Why: Conformance tests are the only systematic check that backends implement the full Protocol; missing a method here means the GitHub backend can ship an incomplete implementation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_server_sam_taskbackend.py` - server-level tests that exercise `TaskBackend` through `server.py`; new `append` routing must be covered | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Server routing tests exist for `create`; missing coverage for `append` would leave the MCP dispatch branch untested\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`sam_plan(action='create')` is the only code path in the SAM MCP server that populates a plan with tasks, and it is monolithic: the entire `tasks_yaml` payload is submitted in a single MCP tool_use call. For large plans (16+ tasks with CLEAR+CoVe-compliant acceptance criteria, verification steps, and rich task bodies) the tool_use block exceeds the model's uninterrupted-streaming window and stalls mid-emission. The tool_use never finishes streaming, so the MCP server never receives the call, and no plan is created.\\n\\nA concrete instance was observed in another repository during Phase-4 task decomposition: the planner emitted `\\\"I have enough context. Now let me create the plan via SAM with all 16 tasks.\\\"` and then idled for 90 seconds before returning `\\\"API Error: Stream idle timeout - partial response received\\\"`. A `sam_plan(action='list')` afterwards returned `count: 0`, confirming no plan was persisted. Total `output_tokens` across the transcript was ~15, ruling out any successful bulk YAML emission.\\n\\n`sam_plan(action='update')` only patches plan-level fields (`context`, `set_fields`) \u2014 it never touches tasks. `sam_task(action='update')` mutates existing tasks but has no create-task code path. `sam_task.append_section` appends markdown inside one task body, not a new task to the plan. There is no incremental-build API.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/server.py:271-296` \u2014 `_sam_plan_create` parses a single `tasks_yaml` string and calls `backend.create_plan(slug, goal, tasks=task_defs, context, issue)` atomically\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/server.py:363-377` \u2014 `_sam_plan_update` calls `backend.update_plan_fields(plan, context, set_fields)`; no task mutation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/server.py:489-542` \u2014 `sam_task` actions are `read|claim|state|update`; the `update` branch handles `set_fields_json` (patches existing task) and `append_section` (appends markdown inside an existing task body) \u2014 no create-task path\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/server.py:388` \u2014 `PlanActionConfig` action literal set: `read | create | list | status | ready | update`\\n</div>\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nEnable agents to build a plan one task at a time so no single MCP tool_use payload ever contains the full plan body.\\n\\nTarget call sequence:\\n\\n1. `sam_plan(action='create', config=CreatePlanConfig(slug=..., goal=..., tasks_yaml='{tasks: []}'))` \u2014 creates an empty plan shell\\n2. `sam_plan(action='append_task', plan='P1', task_yaml=...)` \u2014 appends one task; called once per task\\n3. `sam_plan(action='read', plan='P1')` \u2014 round-trips to the same structure a monolithic create would have produced\\n</div>\\n\\n## Scope Boundary\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nIn scope:\\n\\n- New `TaskBackend.append_task(plan_id, task_def) -> dict` Protocol method on `sam_schema/core/task_backend.py`\\n- Implementations on `local_yaml`, `memory`, and `github_task` backends\\n- New `append_task` action on `sam_plan` wired through the `PlanActionConfig` discriminated union (`sam_schema/core/action_models.py`)\\n- `CreatePlanConfig` accepts an empty `tasks` list without raising\\n- Regression: existing monolithic-create path continues to work unchanged\\n\\nOut of scope:\\n\\n- Client-side streaming-stall fix (this backlog item provides the server-side primitives; client delegation patterns are a separate concern)\\n- Extracting SAM into a standalone plugin (the branch name `add-sam-plan-plugin` is misleading; the diagnosis shifted the remediation target)\\n- Changes to `sam_task` semantics\\n- Changes to on-disk YAML layout beyond what an append-safe writer needs\\n</div>\\n\\n## Non Goals\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Model-side streaming-stall mitigation\\n- Performance optimization of the monolithic path\\n- New backends\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related Observations\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Diagnostic report on the streaming stall (Apr 2026) is not yet captured as an artifact; it is in the session transcript for branch `claude/add-sam-plan-plugin-uZTrQ`\\n- Branch name is misleading \u2014 work target is `plugins/development-harness/`, not a new plugin\\n</div>\\n\\n## Concerns Updated 2026 04 13 Via Re Groom Force\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe following patterns and issues surfaced during the green-phase implementation of #1770 and multiple review rounds on PR #1773. They are recorded here as the authoritative lessons-learned for this item and as seed data for follow-up backlog items.\\n\\n### 1. `AppendTaskConfig.task_yaml: str` is a design smell \u2014 tracked as #1775\\n\\nThe MCP tool takes a YAML string parameter instead of a typed `TaskDefinition`. This forces:\\n- The agent to serialize YAML at the call site\\n- The server to parse YAML in `_sam_plan_append_task`\\n- Schema validation to happen INSIDE the handler rather than at the MCP tool boundary\\n\\nThe whole point of an MCP tool is that its Pydantic config validates at the boundary. Having `task_yaml: str` defeats that. Follow-up: #1775.\\n\\n### 2. Dispatch-discipline rule location \u2014 tracked as #1774\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` contains the rule \\\"every dh dispatch must use `subagent_type='dh:task-worker'`\\\". That rule is in the wrong place \u2014 orchestrators follow skill instructions, not CLAUDE.md. The rule should be inlined at every dispatch step inside every dh skill that delegates. Follow-up: #1774.\\n\\n### 3. Wrong-agent-selection pattern (session recurrence)\\n\\nDuring Phase 4 (swarm-task-planner dispatch), every task in P1770 that edits `.md` files was assigned `python-engineering:python-cli-architect`. That agent is specialized for Python source code. Editing Claude Code agent prompts, skills, and docs requires `plugin-creator:subagent-refactorer` (has `Skill`, `WebFetch`, `mcp__Ref__*` tools, methodology-aware). The mis-assignment was the root cause of the 4 review rounds on this PR:\\n\\n- `f7774c8` \u2014 wrong agent \u2192 wrong format/duplication\\n- `5e04b52` \u2014 wrong agent \u2192 still duplication\\n- `6a162a3` \u2014 wrong agent \u2192 still using prose-based skill invocation\\n- `8dc5a09` \u2014 wrong agent \u2192 \\\"Python projects\\\" constraint insertion across 6 agents\\n- `6655af4` \u2014 wrong agent \u2192 residual inline `Skill()` calls + `P{N}` format errors\\n- `12f769f` \u2014 correct agent \u2192 language-agnostic framing restored across 6 agents\\n- `e286785` \u2014 correct agent \u2192 backend-implementation leaks removed across 9 agents and 5 skills\\n\\nLesson: agent selection must consider the type of content being edited, not just \\\"this agent can edit files\\\". Extension of #1774: inline an agent-selection rubric at dispatch steps.\\n\\n### 4. Python-centric framing leak into a language-agnostic plugin\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` states the plugin is \\\"language-agnostic\\\". Commit `8dc5a09` (wrong agent) inserted \\\"for Python projects\\\" constraints into 6 dh agent role descriptions (codebase-analyzer, plan-validator, integration-checker, dh-context-gathering, feature-researcher, feature-verifier). Commit `12f769f` removed them. The pattern repeats any time an agent from a language-specific plugin edits shared/language-agnostic content.\\n\\n### 5. Backend-implementation leak pattern\\n\\nAgent prose and skill documentation described storage details (\\\"stored in the SAM plan directory\\\", \\\"`dh_paths.plan_dir()`\\\", \\\"context section\\\") when the dh backend is explicitly pluggable (local_yaml, github_task, memory, sqlite per `backend-providers.md`). Commit `e286785` swept 9 agents + 5 skills. The lesson: agents should only know API contracts (tool name, params, return shape), never physical storage.\\n\\n### 6. Duplicated-instruction-plus-skill anti-pattern\\n\\nAgent body prose that says \\\"load this skill\\\" AND then restates what the skill contains is strictly worse than using the `skills:` frontmatter field (auto-loads at dispatch). Three distinct occurrences in this session: dh:create-artifact in feature-researcher/codebase-analyzer/swarm-task-planner, fixed in `6a162a3` and the follow-up sweeps.\\n\\n### 7. `return content inline` template failure for background agents\\n\\nThe original `add-new-feature/SKILL.md` Phase 1-3 templates said \\\"Return the full markdown content in your response\\\" and relied on the orchestrator to call `artifact_register` after. This fails for background-dispatched agents because the task-notification `<result>` field is summarized \u2014 the orchestrator never receives the content. Session workaround was `.tmp/scratch/` disk round-trip. Correct fix landed in `5e04b52` / `8dc5a09`: agents call `artifact_register(content=...)` directly as their final step. The `/dh:create-artifact` skill is the canonical invocation pattern.\\n\\n### 8. Plan ID format confusion\\n\\nDocs referred to plan addresses as `P{N}` as if numeric. Actual format (verified at `sam_schema/core/query.py:35-43`): `\\\"P\\\" + uuid4.hex[:8]` \u2014 e.g., `Pa1b2c3d4`. The `github_task` backend uses the raw issue number as plan_id (no `P` prefix). Legacy numeric IDs are accepted via `_P_NUMERIC_RE` regex for backward compat. Fixed in `8dc5a09` and `6655af4`.\\n\\n### 9. `strict=True` xfail canary CI noise pattern (expected, not a bug)\\n\\nGreen-phase implementation causes xfail tests to transition to xpass(strict) which is a failure condition. Every commit between Wave 2 and Wave 6 triggers Python / Tests + Quality Gate failures on CI. This is intentional per the architect spec (Section 8.1, 8.3) \u2014 the canary signals which markers to remove. Wave 6 T14 removes all 22 markers at once and the CI flips back to green. Reviewers should not chase these intermediate failures.\\n\\n### 10. Task-worker quota oscillation\\n\\nThe green-phase task-workers hit `You're out of extra usage \u00b7 resets 4am (UTC)` on T18 (first attempt) and T6 (first attempt). T18 and T6 both succeeded on retry, and T4-T11 completed without quota errors between them. The pool oscillates rather than hard-exhausting. Session progress continued via retries rather than wait-for-reset.\\n\\n### 11. Codebase-analyzer exploration strategy has Python-centric grep patterns (lines 138-249)\\n\\nNoted as follow-up in `12f769f` commit. The language-agnosticism sweep restored role descriptions but did not fully reframe the exploration patterns (`@pytest.fixture`, `typer.Option`, `*.py` globs, `from typing import Optional`) as language-agnostic illustrations. Needs a deeper restructure \u2014 flagged as a separate grooming pass.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"3594e675a3555d1ee5b20e0f4d3d26b8d42f9dba8b8845da1ef00434e74f3465\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCcN0g\"}"}